            {
              "id": "4a5e8b0a-0c34-3fec-89f0-71e4f3badff1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential for misuse, leading to buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards.\n\nIn the given code snippet, `argc != 3` is not a vulnerability sink. It's a condition that checks if the number of command-line arguments is not equal to 3. However, if the program uses dangerous C functions to handle command-line arguments, it could lead to vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using dangerous C functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ standard library. For example, use `std::string` and its methods for string manipulation instead of `strcpy`, `strcat`, etc. Use `std::cin` and `std::cout` for input/output instead of `scanf` and `printf`.\n\nAlways validate and sanitize input, especially if it comes from an untrusted source like command-line arguments or user input.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWithout the complete source code, it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, here's a general advice:\n\nIf you're using `argv` array (which is usually the case when you're checking `argc`), make sure to validate the arguments before using them. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (argc != 3) {\n    std::cerr << \"Usage: \" << argv[0] << \" arg1 arg2\\n\";\n    return 1;\n}\n\nstd::string arg1 = argv[1];\nstd::string arg2 = argv[2];\n\n// Now use arg1 and arg2 safely\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet doesn't have any library dependencies. However, if you're using `std::string`, `std::cerr`, etc., you need to include the following headers:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n#include <iostream>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
