            {
              "id": "29a7ef67-b4b9-3371-ab6a-f08bc547e654",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, using system time or a static value to seed randomness can lead to predictable random numbers, which is a security vulnerability. This is because the system time is not truly random and can be guessed by an attacker, especially if the attacker knows the time at which the random number generator was seeded. Similarly, a static value will always produce the same sequence of random numbers.\n\nIn the given code snippet, `srand(time(NULL) * world_rank)`, the `srand` function is seeded with the current system time multiplied by a static value `world_rank`. This can lead to predictable random numbers and potential security vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a source of entropy that is hard for an attacker to predict. For example, you could use a hardware random number generator if one is available. Alternatively, you could use a cryptographically secure pseudo-random number generator (CSPRNG) that is designed to be unpredictable and resistant to cryptographic attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `srand` and `rand`, use `std::random_device` and `std::mt19937` from the `<random>` library, which are designed to produce high-quality random numbers. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_int_distribution<> dis(1, 6);\n\nint random_number = dis(gen);\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is a uniformly-distributed integer random number generator that produces non-deterministic random numbers. `std::mt19937` is a Mersenne Twister pseudo-random generator of 32-bit numbers with a state size of 19937 bits.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<random>`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-330: Use of Insufficiently Random Values](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/330.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS149435"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "931d06e5-76f2-3710-bf5d-3ef19ccff0cf",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that arises when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, memory leaks, or other types of undefined behavior. In this case, the `qsort` function is used, which is not inherently unsafe, but can lead to vulnerabilities if not used correctly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use C++ standard library functions and algorithms that are safer and less prone to errors. For instance, instead of using `qsort`, you can use `std::sort` from the `<algorithm>` library, which is type-safe and does not require a function pointer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <vector>\n\n// Assuming rand_nums is a vector of floats\nstd::sort(rand_nums.begin(), rand_nums.end());\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>` for `qsort`\n- `<algorithm>` for `std::sort`\n- `<vector>` for `std::vector`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are active and accessible for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "63deec87-6afe-3450-bc4f-5076a07f89f9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflow attacks. In the provided code snippet, the function `atoi()` is used, which is a prohibited C function. The `atoi()` function converts a string to an integer and it does not perform any error checking. If the input string cannot be converted into an integer, it returns zero, which can lead to unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using prohibited C functions that do not perform error checking or that are known to be insecure. Instead, use safer alternatives that are available. In the case of `atoi()`, a safer alternative would be `strtol()`, which provides error checking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoi()` function with `strtol()`. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\nchar *end;\nlong numbers_per_proc = strtol(argv[1], &end, 10);\nif (end == argv[1] || *end != '\\0' || errno == ERANGE) {\n    // handle the error\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `strtol()` will set `end` to point to the first invalid character in the string or to the null byte at the end of the string if the entire string is valid. If `end` points to the start of the string, it means the string could not be converted to a number. If `end` does not point to the null byte, it means the string contains invalid characters after a valid number. If `errno` is set to `ERANGE`, it means the number is out of range.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdlib.h`: for `strtol()` and `atoi()`\n- `errno.h`: for `errno`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d4be272d-0edb-3127-9b8b-c1d6e00ed0fc",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of functions like `rand()` to generate random numbers. These functions are not suitable for generating random numbers in a security context because they are predictable and can be easily reproduced. This can lead to various security issues, such as predictability in password generation, session identifiers, or cryptographic keys.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a strong cryptographic random number generator. In C++, you can use functions provided by libraries like `<random>` which are designed to produce high-quality random numbers suitable for cryptographic use.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a code fix for the specific vulnerability sink:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_real_distribution<> dis(0, 1);\n\nfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n    random_numbers[i] = dis(gen);\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is a uniformly-distributed integer random number generator that produces non-deterministic random numbers. `std::mt19937` is a Mersenne Twister pseudo-random generator of 32-bit numbers with a state size of 19937 bits. `std::uniform_real_distribution` produces random floating-point numbers in the range [0, 1).\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264",
                  "d4be272d-0edb-3127-9b8b-c1d6e00ed0fc"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "dc545dab-61e0-3b5b-a466-43d24a03dd28",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the use of non-cryptographic or weak random number generators can lead to vulnerabilities. This is because these generators can produce predictable values, which can be exploited by an attacker to predict future values that will be returned by the generator. This can lead to a variety of security issues, such as the ability to predict session identifiers, passwords, or other sensitive data.\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink in this case is `r == RAND_MAX`. The `RAND_MAX` is a constant in C++ which specifies the maximum value that can be returned by the `rand()` function. If `r` is equal to `RAND_MAX`, it means that the maximum possible value has been returned by the `rand()` function, which could indicate a problem with the randomness of the values being generated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a cryptographic random number generator instead of a non-cryptographic or weak one. Cryptographic random number generators are designed to produce values that are not predictable, making them more secure for use in applications where the randomness of the values is important.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `rand()`, use a function like `rand_r()` which is reentrant and thus safer. Alternatively, you can use functions from the `<random>` library in C++11 and later, which provides a variety of generators and distributions to choose from.\n\nHere is an example of how to use the `<random>` library to generate random numbers:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;  // Will be used to obtain a seed for the random number engine\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd()); // Standard mersenne_twister_engine seeded with rd()\nstd::uniform_int_distribution<> distrib(1, 6);\n\nint r = distrib(gen);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c6bdd744-26e9-3f31-afbf-981d99698307",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of functions like `rand()` to generate random numbers. These functions are not suitable for generating random numbers in a security context because they are predictable and can be easily reproduced. This can lead to various security issues, such as predictability in password generation, session identifiers, or cryptographic keys.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a strong cryptographic random number generator. In C++, you can use functions provided by libraries like `<random>` which are designed to produce high-quality random numbers suitable for cryptographic use.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a code fix for the specific vulnerability sink:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_real_distribution<> dis(0, 1);\n\nfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n    random_numbers[i] = dis(gen);\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is a uniformly-distributed integer random number generator that produces non-deterministic random numbers. `std::mt19937` is a Mersenne Twister pseudo-random generator of 32-bit numbers with a state size of 19937 bits. `std::uniform_real_distribution` produces random floating-point numbers in the range [0, 1).\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
