            {
              "id": "54e2337d-826e-39a6-a1e4-786267323a92",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. These functions are often prohibited because they do not perform bounds checking or input validation, which can lead to vulnerabilities if not handled properly.\n\nIn the given sink `argc > 4`, there is no direct use of a prohibited C function. However, if `argc` (which represents the number of command-line arguments passed to the program) is not properly validated, it could potentially lead to issues such as array index out of bounds, if these arguments are used to access array elements, for example.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this, you should always validate the number of command-line arguments passed to your program. If your program expects a certain number of arguments, make sure to check that this exact number of arguments is passed. If not, the program should exit with an appropriate error message.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the given sink:\n\n```cpp\nif (argc != 5) {\n    std::cerr << \"Error: Invalid number of arguments.\\n\";\n    return 1;\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, the program checks if the number of arguments is exactly 5 (including the program name itself). If not, it prints an error message and exits with a non-zero status code.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not require any additional library dependencies.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Proactive Controls](https://owasp.org/www-project-proactive-controls/)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-20: Improper Input Validation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-134: Use of Externally-Controlled Format String](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/134.html)\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "38a50e35-7edc-3667-b48f-1367fed77937",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards. \n\nIn the given sink `argc > 3`, there is no direct use of a prohibited C function. However, `argc` is a parameter in the `main` function that represents the number of command-line arguments passed to the program. If not properly validated, it can lead to vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this type of vulnerability, avoid using dangerous C functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. \n\nFor the specific sink `argc > 3`, ensure that the number of command-line arguments passed to the program is within the expected range. Validate the `argc` value before using it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the specific sink:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n\nint main(int argc, char* argv[]) {\n    if(argc <= 3) {\n        std::cout << \"Expected more than 3 arguments.\\n\";\n        return 1;\n    }\n    // Rest of the code\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we check if `argc` is less than or equal to 3. If it is, we print an error message and return 1 to indicate an error. This way, we ensure that the program only proceeds if the correct number of arguments is provided.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not require any additional library dependencies beyond the standard C++ library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4d4a84e0-76c3-3401-a8dc-5e5f9766542f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, memory corruption, or other vulnerabilities. These functions are often part of the C standard library and are included for backwards compatibility, but their use is discouraged in modern programming.\n\nIn the case of `argc > 2`, this is not a vulnerability in itself, but it could potentially lead to vulnerabilities if the additional command line arguments are not properly handled. For example, if these arguments are used to construct file paths or SQL queries without proper sanitization, it could lead to path traversal or SQL injection vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this type of vulnerability, avoid using unsafe C functions in your C++ code. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ standard library. For example, instead of using `strcpy()`, use `std::string`.\n\nIn the case of `argc > 2`, ensure that all command line arguments are properly sanitized before they are used. This could involve checking that file paths do not contain directory traversal sequences (e.g., `../`), or using parameterized queries for SQL.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf your program requires more than two command line arguments, you should handle them in a safe manner. Here is an example of how you might do this:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nint main(int argc, char* argv[]) {\n    if (argc > 2) {\n        std::vector<std::string> args(argv + 1, argv + argc);\n        // Now args contains all command line arguments as std::strings.\n        // You can process them in a safe manner.\n    } else {\n        std::cerr << \"Error: Too few arguments.\\n\";\n        return 1;\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nThis code uses the `std::vector` and `std::string` classes from the C++ standard library, which handle memory management automatically and are generally safer than their C counterparts.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThis code requires the following libraries:\n\n- iostream\n- vector\n- string\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2f41e2ad-f547-35e5-96fd-d8f25790bb53",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards due to their potential misuse or abuse.\n\nIn the given code sink `argc > 1`, there is no direct use of a prohibited C function. However, `argc` is a parameter in the `main` function that represents the number of command-line arguments passed to the program. If not properly validated, it can lead to vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this potential vulnerability, you should always validate the command-line arguments (`argc` and `argv`) before using them. Ensure that the number of arguments (`argc`) is as expected and that the arguments themselves (`argv`) are valid and safe to use.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how you can validate `argc` and `argv`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n\nint main(int argc, char* argv[]) {\n    if (argc != 2) {\n        std::cerr << \"Usage: \" << argv[0] << \" <input>\\n\";\n        return 1;\n    }\n\n    // Now it's safe to use argv[1]\n    std::cout << \"Input: \" << argv[1] << \"\\n\";\n\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, the program expects exactly one command-line argument (in addition to the program name itself). If the number of arguments is not as expected, the program prints a usage message and exits with a non-zero status code.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code example requires the following library:\n\n- iostream\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "451dfcd6-9df6-3166-ad4b-71e7730a6687",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the use of non-cryptographic or weak random number generators can lead to vulnerabilities. This is because these generators can produce predictable outcomes, which can be exploited by attackers. The `rand()` function in C++ is a non-cryptographic random number generator, which means it is not suitable for generating random numbers in a security context.\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink in the provided code is `col = rand() % N`. This line of code generates a random number using `rand()`, then uses the modulus operator `%` to limit the range of the random number to `N`. However, the `rand()` function is not a secure way to generate random numbers, and the modulus operation can introduce bias, making certain outcomes more likely than others.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use a cryptographic random number generator instead of `rand()`. In C++, you can use the `<random>` library, which provides a variety of random number generators that are more secure and less predictable than `rand()`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_int_distribution<> dis(0, N-1);\n\ncol = dis(gen);\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is a uniformly-distributed integer random number generator that produces non-deterministic random numbers. `std::mt19937` is a Mersenne Twister random number generator, and `std::uniform_int_distribution` produces random integers in a range [a, b].\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `<random>` library is required by the code example to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264",
                  "451dfcd6-9df6-3166-ad4b-71e7730a6687"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "545cde3d-4e0c-32b9-bf6e-b7d05b14c83f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the use of non-cryptographic or weak random number generators can lead to vulnerabilities. This is because these generators can produce predictable outcomes, which can be exploited by attackers. The `rand()` function in C++ is a non-cryptographic random number generator, which means it is not suitable for generating random numbers in a security context.\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink in the provided code is `col = rand() % N`. This line of code generates a random number using `rand()`, then uses the modulus operator `%` to limit the range of the random number to `N`. However, the `rand()` function is not a secure way to generate random numbers, and the modulus operation can introduce bias, making certain outcomes more likely than others.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use a cryptographic random number generator instead of `rand()`. In C++, you can use the `<random>` library, which provides a variety of random number generators that are more secure and less predictable than `rand()`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_int_distribution<> dis(0, N-1);\n\ncol = dis(gen);\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is a uniformly-distributed integer random number generator that produces non-deterministic random numbers. `std::mt19937` is a Mersenne Twister random number generator, and `std::uniform_int_distribution` produces random integers in a range [a, b].\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `<random>` library is required by the code example to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
