                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards. \n\nIn the given sink `argc > 3`, there is no direct use of a prohibited C function. However, `argc` is a parameter in the `main` function that represents the number of command-line arguments passed to the program. If not properly validated, it can lead to vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this type of vulnerability, avoid using dangerous C functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. \n\nFor the specific sink `argc > 3`, ensure that the number of command-line arguments passed to the program is within the expected range. Validate the `argc` value before using it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the specific sink:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n\nint main(int argc, char* argv[]) {\n    if(argc <= 3) {\n        std::cout << \"Expected more than 3 arguments.\\n\";\n        return 1;\n    }\n    // Rest of the code\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we check if `argc` is less than or equal to 3. If it is, we print an error message and return 1 to indicate an error. This way, we ensure that the program only proceeds if the correct number of arguments is provided.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not require any additional library dependencies beyond the standard C++ library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
